<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<odoo>

    <!-- THE LOGIN PAGE IS NOT A PAGE OF THE CLIENT'S WEBSITE.

         THE DEFECT, measured on a 36-app database with `website` installed:
         /web/login served our branded login card wrapped in Odoo's default
         website chrome — a "Your Logo" navbar with Shop / Events / Courses /
         Jobs / Contact us, and a footer carrying "Useful Links", "We are a
         team of passionate people...", info@yourcompany.example.com and
         +1 555-555-5556. That is the first screen a prospect sees on the
         product's own shipped configuration.

         Every login assertion we had still passed, because every one of them
         measured the CARD: login_powered_sapian=1, login_mark=1,
         login_signup=0, login_primary=#14454F. None measured the page around
         it. Same family as the proxy rule — we asserted the part we built and
         never the page we serve.

         THE CAUSE — website/views/website_templates.xml:1623, an ordinary view
         inheritance, NOT route dispatch. Nothing in website's `ir.http` or its
         controllers mentions /web/login; the route is still auth_signup's, and
         it still renders `web.login`. What changes is the LAYOUT:

             <template id="login_layout" inherit_id="web.login_layout"
                       name="Website Login Layout" priority="20">
                 <xpath expr="t[@t-call]" position="replace">
                     <t t-call="website.layout">
                         <div class="oe_website_login_container" t-out="0"/>
                     </t>
                 </xpath>
             </template>

         `web.login_layout` is a `t-call` to `web.frontend_layout` carrying four
         t-sets, two of which are `no_header = True` and `no_footer = True`.
         `web.frontend_layout` draws its header and footer as
         `<header t-if="not no_header">` / `<footer t-if="not no_footer">`, so
         the stock login page has neither.

         website REPLACES that entire `t-call` element — and the replacement
         carries NO t-sets. `website.layout` inherits `portal.frontend_layout`
         which inherits `web.frontend_layout`, and it modifies the header and
         footer with `position="attributes"` rather than replacing them, so both
         are still guarded by exactly those two flags. The flags simply stopped
         being set. The chrome is not added by website; it is UNSUPPRESSED by
         website, which is why nothing looked wrong in any template we own.

         THE FIX — deactivate that one inheritance. `web.login_layout` then
         renders as it does on a database with no website at all, so the page is
         the same page in both configurations rather than merely being chrome-
         free in each. An application login is not a page of the marketing site,
         and it should not change because the client bought an e-commerce
         module.

         WHY DEACTIVATE, and not re-set the two flags. Adding
         `no_header`/`no_footer` back inside website's `t-call` would also hide
         the chrome, and it was rejected for two reasons. It leaves the page
         rendering through `website.layout`, so the card that `web.login_layout`
         provides stays destroyed and the page still differs between the two
         configurations — chrome-free, but not the same page. And it depends on
         the exact markup of website's replacement, so an upstream edit to that
         one xpath silently changes what we are patching. Deactivation depends
         only on the view's identity.

         IT SURVIVES A `website` UPGRADE, from the loader's own rule rather than
         from hope: odoo/tools/convert.py:517-524 writes `active` only when the
         template tag carries an `active` attribute AND the record is new or the
         mode is not update. website's `login_layout` declares no `active`, so
         `-u website` never touches the column. This record re-asserts it on
         every update of THIS module regardless, so a database that was ever
         changed by hand self-heals.

         WHAT ELSE THIS COVERS — the same one line, because they share the
         layout, not because they were separately handled:
           * /web/signup           auth_signup_login_templates.xml:36, t-call
                                   web.login_layout
           * /web/reset_password   the same file, line 72, likewise
         Both are asserted below by `TestLoginPageIsNotAWebsitePage`.

         WHAT IT DOES NOT COVER, deliberately: the customer PORTAL (/my/...)
         renders through `portal.frontend_layout` and `website.layout` and never
         touches `web.login_layout`. It keeps the website's header and footer,
         and it should — the portal IS the client's customer-facing area, where
         their own branding is the correct answer. Verified by fetching /my on
         the same database: the chrome is present before and after this change.

         One website feature does share this layout: `website.protected_403`
         (website_templates.xml:2832), the password prompt for a protected
         website page, `t-call="website.login_layout"`. The xmlid still
         resolves — a t-call by xmlid renders the root view's combined arch, and
         deactivating an extension removes its contribution, not the record.
         Measured over HTTP on a page with a visibility password, because a bare
         `_render` of that template fails for want of a request in EITHER state
         and settles nothing:

             login_layout=ACTIVE    http=403  prompt=1  chrome=1  bytes=24333
             login_layout=INACTIVE  http=403  prompt=1  chrome=0  bytes=6531

         It keeps working, and it loses the chrome too. That is a real
         consequence and it is stated rather than buried: any change to this
         shared layout reaches that prompt, and scoping it to the auth routes
         alone would need a flag passed from the controller. Guarded by
         `TestProtectedPageStillRenders`. -->

    <record id="website.login_layout" model="ir.ui.view">
        <field name="active" eval="False"/>
    </record>

</odoo>
